@@s001 Code: BFS in Java
@@s002 Code: BFS in Java
@@s003 The complete BFS: distances from a source on an unweighted graph. Every line explained, and the syntax notes needed to write it.
@@s004 BFS with the queue
@@s005 The queue is an <code>ArrayDeque</code>: <code>add</code> puts at the back, <code>poll</code> removes from the front. <code>dist[v] = -1</code> means "not yet seen", and the node is marked when it enters the queue.
@@s006 BFS with the queue
@@s007 The nodes are numbered from 0 to n−1, so I can use them as array indices.
@@s008 All distances start from −1 (not seen). <code>Arrays.fill</code> fills the array in one step.
@@s009 A <code>Deque</code> used as a FIFO queue. <code>Integer</code> and not <code>int</code>: collections contain objects.
@@s010 The source is at distance 0. It enters the queue already marked as seen.
@@s011 I extract the first node. <code>poll</code> returns <code>null</code> if the queue is empty, but the <code>while</code> prevents this.
@@s012 I scan the neighbours of <code>u</code> with a <i>for-each</i>: <code>v</code> takes each value of the list.
@@s013 Only the neighbours not yet seen: the first arrival is also the shortest path.
@@s014 Distance = distance of <code>u</code> plus one.
@@s015 I put <code>v</code> in the queue: it will be extracted after the nodes already in the queue, so layer by layer.
@@s016 Syntax notes
@@s017 <code>Integer</code> → <code>int</code> in <code>int u = coda.poll();</code>: the automatic conversion is called <i>unboxing</i>.
@@s018 <code>ArrayDeque</code> is faster than <code>LinkedList</code> as a queue; it does not accept <code>null</code>.
@@s019 <code>StringBuilder</code> accumulates the text and prints it only once: printing line by line on large outputs is slow.
@@s020 Running it
